
  [1m2. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m — pushing back.[0m This would trade a bug I just fixed for a worse one. The window math is [38;5;153mnow - [39m
  [38;5;153mself.window_seconds[39m against stored timestamps, so it depends on the clock being monotonic. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m is not: an NTP
  step backwards makes [38;5;153mcutoff[39m move backwards too, so old events stop expiring and the limiter throttles traffic it
  should admit; a step forwards expires the whole window at once and lets a full burst through. That's exactly what the
  class docstring at [38;5;153mlimiter.py:8-10[39m was written to prevent, so the current choice is deliberate, not an oversight.

  The underlying need — correlating limiter events with wall-clock log timestamps — is legitimate and I can do it
  without breaking the window. Two options:

  - Keep [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m for the deque, and have [38;5;153mallow()[39m return/record a [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m value alongside it purely for
    logging. The two are captured microseconds apart, so they correlate fine.
  - Store [38;5;153m(monotonic, wall)[39m pairs in the deque, expire on the monotonic field, and expose the wall field to log
    consumers.

  Tell me which you want (or if there's a different consumer of these timestamps I'm not seeing) and I'll implement it.
  One caveat: on Linux, [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m excludes suspend time, so a host that sleeps will hold events in the window
  longer than wall-clock would. [38;5;153mtime.perf_counter()[39m has the same property. If suspend-spanning behavior matters for the
  gateway, that's a real discussion — but [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m isn't the fix for it.

  [1m3. Pluggable storage backend — pushing back on YAGNI grounds.[0m Nothing in the repo references Redis or any second
  backend: [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m has [38;5;153mdependencies = [][39m, and there's one caller shape and one implementation. More importantly,
  an interface extracted from the current deque wouldn't survive contact with Redis. This code does read-modify-write in
  three steps (scan, [38;5;153mpopleft[39m, [38;5;153mappend[39m) under the implicit safety of a single process. A correct Redis sliding window has
  to be atomic on the server — [38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m + [38;5;153mZCARD[39m + [38;5;153mZADD[39m in one Lua script or [38;5;153mMULTI[39m — because otherwise
  concurrent gateway instances race between the count and the add and blow past the limit. So a [38;5;153mStorage[39m protocol with
  deque-shaped methods ([38;5;153mtrim[39m, [38;5;153mcount[39m, [38;5;153mappend[39m) would encode the wrong contract and get rewritten the day Redis actually
  lands.

  If Redis is genuinely on the roadmap, the useful abstraction is one method — [38;5;153mtry_acquire(key, limit, window) -> bool[39m —
  with the whole check-and-admit decision behind it, so each backend can make it atomic its own way. I'd rather add
  that when there's a second implementation to validate it against than guess now. Say the word if you want it
  regardless and I'll build that shape.

